```


`tricycle` infers cell cycle position by projecting each cell's gene expression into a 2D reference
embedding by using pre-calculated PCA loadings. When cycling cells are present, this results in a 
continuous "loop" of cells around an origin. The origin can be specified manually or estimated from the data.
Cell-cycle position is then calculated with reference to the origin, giving
`tricycle_theta` which takes values $[0, 2\pi)$ in radians (i.e. 0 to 360 
degrees).

## Expression around theta

For known cell cycle markers, expression is averaged across cells in angular bins around the origin.
The right panel showes these averaged expressions as a function of theta.
The lines are circular LOESS smooths of bin means, weighted by their cell counts.
Colours group the canonical markers by their expected cell-cycle phase; CDKN1A (gene encoding p21 which acts as
inhibitor of cyclin-dependent kinase complexes stopping cell cycle progression at the G1/S and G2/M transitions)
is shown neutrally as a checkpoint diagnostic.

The left panel provides the corresponding pooled tricycle projection coloured
by theta. Grey radial lines mark tricycle's approximate phase boundaries:
G1/G0 wraps from $1.75\pi$ through zero to $0.25\pi$, G1/S spans
$0.25\pi$-$0.5\pi$, S spans $0.5\pi$-$\pi$, and G2/M spans
$\pi$-$1.75\pi$. The grey vertical lines mark the same boundaries on the
expression plot (right panel).

```{r expression_curves, fig.height=6, fig.width=14}
plot_cell_cycle_expression(report_data)
```

## Pooled projection and estimated origin

The panels show the cell-cycle projection and the origin used to calculate
theta. The left panel shows cell density; the right panel shows downsampled cells such that density across the embedding is equal. The black cross marks the chosen
origin, and the grey path shows how its estimate changes across target
proportions. Grey radial lines mark the phase boundaries described
above. With a manual origin, the estimated alternatives are shown for comparison [text should the be different whether you select a manual origin or not?].

Compare the alternatives in `cell_cycle.cyc_density_percentile_grid` before
choosing `cell_cycle.cyc_density_percentile`. For example, `1.3` sets the density
threshold at the 1.3rd percentile across cells. Cells at or below that density
receive full weight; denser cells receive less weight, in inverse proportion to
their density. Tied densities can make the percentage at full weight larger
than requested. At `0`, weights are proportional to inverse density; at `100`,
every cell has equal weight. This setting does not specify the percentage of
cycling cells or discard cells from the analysis. Alternatively, set
`cell_cycle.cyc_origin_manual` to specify the origin coordinates manually.

```{r pooled_projection, fig.height=5.5, fig.width=11}
plot_cell_cycle_pooled_projection(report_data)
```

## Spatial marker expression

Observed counts for the canonical cycling genes were captured from the full
run-level matrices, library-size normalized to 10,000, and log-transformed.
Each gene is
coloured relative to its own 98th percentile among expressing cells, so colour
intensity is comparable spatially within a gene but not between genes. Cells
are drawn in a reproducibly randomized order. The facet title reports the
number of cells with expression above zero.

```{r spatial_expression, fig.height=8, fig.width=10}
plot_cell_cycle_spatial_expression(report_data)
```

## Sample-level diagnostics {.tabset}

Point size represents the capped inverse-density inclusion probability used by
the origin estimator. Cells outside the dense non-cycling blob generally reach
the maximum size of 2, while cells in the blob are drawn smaller. Theta remains
encoded by colour.

```{r sample_diagnostics, fig.height=5, fig.width=10, results='asis'}
render_cell_cycle_sample_diagnostics(report_data)
```

## Approximate phase percentages by sample

Tricycle is designed to represent cell cycle continuously. For an approximate
summary only, the plot uses the angular sectors introduced with the pooled
projection above. G1/G0 is omitted because it dominates the percentages in
predominantly non-cycling samples. The remaining values are still percentages
of all QC-passed cells and are not renormalized.

```{r phase_proportions, fig.height=5, fig.width=6}
phase_summary = make_cell_cycle_phase_summary(report_data)
print(phase_summary$plot)
knitr::kable(phase_summary$table, digits = 1,
  caption = 'Approximate non-G1/G0 phase percentages')
```


